Short:        WebKit browser OWB 2.1 for 68k OS3.x
Author:       OWB: Fabien Coeurjoly, Jacek Piszczek, Krzysztof Smiechowicz;
              68k port: TheMamboMan
Uploader:     TheMamboMan
Type:         comm/www
Version:      2.1-m68k (early test release 1)
Architecture: m68k-amigaos
Requires:     68020+ with FPU, RTG, MUI, AmiSSL 5.27+, TCP/IP, 256MB+ RAM

Odyssey Web Browser (OWB) for classic AmigaOS 3.x
=================================================

This is Odyssey Web Browser, the WebKit-based browser from MorphOS, AROS
and AmigaOS 4, cross-compiled for classic 68k AmigaOS 3.x for the first
time. It is based on WebKitGTK 2.24.4 (February 2019). It renders modern
sites with real HTML5, CSS and JavaScript, and connects over HTTPS
(TLS 1.3) through AmiSSL.

EARLY TEST RELEASE
------------------

This is a first release to get it into people's hands. It works, but it
is slow and needs a lot of memory. It is built without the full compiler
optimisation (-O1), and the speed should improve in later releases.

Timings in WinUAE (68020 + 68881 FPU, JIT):

  Window open after double-click     about 9 seconds
  duckduckgo.com home page           about 8 seconds
  DuckDuckGo search results          about 70 seconds
  en.wikipedia.org article           20-45 seconds
  aminet.net                         about 7 seconds

On real hardware, even a 68060, expect it to be much slower than this.

Please report problems with a description of what you did, the site,
and any Guru code. Include whether you use WinUAE or real hardware, your
CPU and memory size, and your TCP/IP stack.

Requirements
------------

- AmigaOS 3.x with a 68020 or better and an FPU (68881/68882, or the
  FPU built into the 68040/68060)
- Lots of fast RAM: 256 MB or more. Tested with 384 MB.
- MUI (tested with MUI 5)
- AmiSSL 5.27 or newer, for https
- A TCP/IP stack with bsdsocket.library
- An RTG graphics card with Picasso96 or CyberGraphX. Running on
  AGA or ECS screens without RTG has not been tested.

Tested only on AmiKit XE in WinUAE 5.3.1: 68020 + 68881 FPU with JIT,
UAEgfx RTG, 384 MB. Not yet tested on real hardware, on 68030, 68040 or
68060 CPUs, without RTG, with Roadshow, or on a plain Workbench setup.
Reports welcome.

Installation
------------

1. Unpack the archive anywhere, for example to Work:.
2. Double-click the OWB icon. The icon sets the stack to 64,000,000
   bytes, which OWB needs.

To start OWB from a Shell instead, set the stack first:

  stack 64000000
  OWB

Fonts are included in the Fonts drawer. No download is needed.

What works
----------

- Pages with HTML5, CSS and JavaScript: DuckDuckGo, Wikipedia,
  Aminet and others
- HTTPS through AmiSSL
- Typing in forms, the caret, arrow keys and Backspace
- Search suggestions
- Tabs, back and forward, history and the URL completion list
- Cookies and local storage, kept between sessions
- Downloads (left-click a link to a file)
- Clean exit with several tabs open

Known issues
------------

- Slow. JavaScript-heavy pages can take a minute or more.
- Right-clicking does not open a context menu.
- file:/// URLs do not open local files.
- Running out of memory can crash the whole system instead of showing
  an error. Keep plenty of free fast RAM.
- Memory use creeps up slowly when reloading pages.
- No video or audio playback in this build.
- YouTube does not work: its pages stop loading partway (also from the
  search bar's YouTube option). Other search engines work.
- The URL box may complete a typed address to one from your history.
  Press Del before Enter to remove the completion.

Source code and building
------------------------

The source code for this port, with build instructions:

  https://github.com/themamboman/OdysseyWebBrowser-m68k

The AmigaOS 3.x threading library it uses:

  https://github.com/themamboman/aos_multithreading_library

Built with the AmigaPorts m68k-amigaos-gcc (GCC 16) and clib2.

Making it run on 68k needed many fixes, mostly in the toolchain, the C
library and the third-party libraries, not in OWB itself. A few
examples:

- NaN comparisons, log10, fmod, pow and other maths that clib2 got wrong
- SQLite on AmigaDOS: seeking past the end of a file fails, unlike on
  Unix, which broke cookies and history
- WebKit threads must be DOS processes, with their own bsdsocket and
  AmiSSL bases
- JavaScriptCore assumed 4-byte alignment for pointers, but m68k GCC only
  aligns them to 2 bytes: a crash that only appeared when the code
  layout shifted

